@anon.9efbc0c128adab6190fabe7f7e045142.1079 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ [1180 x i8] }> <{ [1180 x i8] c"A filter for selecting elements within the document.\0A\0AYou can construct a selector in the following ways:\0A- you can use an element [function]\0A- you can filter for an element function with\0A  [specific fields]($function.where)\0A- you can use a [string]($str) or [regular expression]($regex)\0A- you can use a [`{<label>}`]($label)\0A- you can use a [`location`]\0A- call the [`selector`] constructor to convert any of the above types into a\0A  selector value and use the methods below to refine it\0A\0ASelectors are used to [apply styling rules]($styling/#show-rules) to\0Aelements. You can also use selectors to [query] the document for certain\0Atypes of elements.\0A\0AFurthermore, you can pass a selector to several of Typst's built-in\0Afunctions to configure their behaviour. One such example is the [outline]\0Awhere it can be used to change which elements are listed within the outline.\0A\0AMultiple selectors can be combined using the methods shown below. However,\0Anot all kinds of selectors are supported in all places, at the moment.\0A\0A# Example\0A```example\0A#context query(\0A  heading.where(level: 1)\0A    .or(heading.where(level: 2))\0A)\0A\0A= This will be found\0A== So will this\0A=== But this will not.\0A```" }>, align 1
